---
registryId: memcached
---

{% section-card tone="intro" %}
## What is Memcached?

Memcached is a networked, in-memory key-value cache. An application gives it an opaque
key, a value, and usually an expiration time; a later request for that key can avoid a
slower database query, API call, or rendering step.

Its most important invariant is simple: **every cached value must be disposable**.
Memcached can evict an item, expire it, lose it with a server, or return a miss after the
client remaps a key. The application must still be correct by reading an authoritative
source and rebuilding the value.

Memcached is deliberately narrower than a database. It does not provide durable
transactions, query indexes, a replication log, or application-level cache coherence.
That small contract makes it fast, but it leaves routing, refill, invalidation, and
failure containment with the application.
{% /section-card %}

{% concept-grid columns="4" %}
{% concept-card title="Opaque key and value" eyebrow="Data model" icon="key" tone="blue" %}
The server does not understand rows, JSON fields, relationships, or business versions.
The client serializes a bounded value and chooses a stable key.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Memory is a budget" eyebrow="Capacity" icon="memory" tone="cyan" %}
Values occupy slab-class chunks. Expiration and eviction keep use bounded, while item
shape and access frequency determine what remains resident.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Clients own placement" eyebrow="Distribution" icon="route" tone="violet" %}
A client library commonly hashes each key to one server. Consistent hashing limits how
many keys move when the pool changes.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Misses are normal" eyebrow="Correctness" icon="repeat" tone="green" %}
Every read path needs a bounded source fallback. Hit rate changes latency and source
load, but it must never decide whether the answer is correct.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

{% section-card accent="blue" %}
## Put Memcached on a cache-aside request path

In the common **cache-aside** pattern, the application reads Memcached first. A hit is
returned immediately. A miss causes the application to read the source, serialize the
result, store a temporary copy, and return the source result. The source remains the
authority throughout the flow.

{% system-flow title="One cache-aside read" caption="A cache hit ends at Memcached. A miss temporarily joins the source path, then repopulates the disposable copy." %}
{% system-node title="Application" eyebrow="Name the object" icon="workflow" tone="blue" %}
Build a namespaced, versioned key from the request and choose a bounded timeout for the
cache lookup.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Client routing" eyebrow="Choose one node" icon="route" tone="violet" %}
Hash the key against the current server pool. Connection pooling and failure detection
belong to the client or an explicitly deployed proxy.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Memcached" eyebrow="Fast optional copy" icon="memory" tone="cyan" %}
Return the opaque bytes on a hit. On a miss, report absence rather than discovering or
loading the source value itself.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Authoritative source" eyebrow="Correct fallback" icon="database" tone="amber" %}
Read the database or service, enforce its consistency rules, and rebuild the cached
representation under a refill-ownership contract.
{% /system-node %}
{% /system-flow %}

### Keep the miss path bounded

- Set a short cache timeout and treat timeout, connection failure, and miss as distinct
  telemetry even when all three fall back to the source.
- Limit concurrent source reads so an unavailable cache cannot turn into an unlimited
  database fan-out.
- Decide whether callers wait, serve a stale local value, degrade a feature, or fail
  when the source is already saturated.
- Add TTL jitter so many related keys do not expire on the same second.
- Coordinate rebuilds for hot keys with single-flight work or the meta protocol's
  recache ownership features.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="cyan" %}
## Size memory and miss capacity together

A cache is useful only when the resident values match the access distribution and the
source can survive the remaining misses. Begin with explicit quantities instead of a
target hit-rate slogan.

{% metric-strip %}
{% metric label="Working set" value="items x item bytes" detail="Include keys, item metadata, serialization, and slab fit" tone="violet" /%}
{% metric label="Usable memory" value="nodes x RAM x efficiency" detail="Reserve room for allocation behavior and non-item overhead" tone="cyan" /%}
{% metric label="Miss load" value="traffic x (1 - hit rate)" detail="Budget source concurrency, latency, and retry amplification" tone="rose" /%}
{% metric label="Node pressure" value="operations / live nodes" detail="Measure skew and hot keys, not only an even average" tone="amber" /%}
{% /metric-strip %}

The lab uses transparent planning assumptions. Change the object shape and target hit
rate separately: memory coverage does not predict popularity, while a high measured hit
rate does not prove that the source can survive a cold restart.

{% interactive-block id="technology/memcached-performance" dataFile="/api/content/technology/memcached/data/capacity-envelope.json" /%}

{% code-block file="/api/content/technology/memcached/code/plan-cache-envelope.py" language="python" title="Estimate memory coverage and miss load" /%}

### Benchmark what the formula cannot know

- Measure p50, p95, and maximum serialized value sizes; an average hides expensive slab
  classes and network outliers.
- Record key popularity. A small number of hot keys can produce a high hit rate while
  overloading one node or one network path.
- Benchmark the exact client, protocol, connection reuse, value codec, TLS mode, and
  target hardware.
- Remove one node during peak traffic and observe source request rate, tail latency,
  timeouts, and refill duration.
- Repeat after a full cold start; planned steady-state hit rate is unavailable during
  warm-up.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="violet" %}
## Understand slabs, expiration, and eviction

Memcached assigns items to size classes. Each class receives pages and divides them
into fixed-size chunks. An item consumes one chunk from the nearest fitting class, so
the difference between item bytes and chunk bytes becomes internal waste. Each class
behaves like a smaller cache with its own pressure and LRU activity.

{% concept-grid columns="3" %}
{% concept-card title="Expiration" eyebrow="Freshness boundary" icon="clock" tone="blue" %}
A TTL says when the cached copy becomes invalid. In the basic text protocol, values up
to 30 days are relative seconds; larger values are interpreted as Unix timestamps.
Expiration is not a durable deletion guarantee.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Reclaim" eyebrow="Expired space" icon="repeat" tone="green" %}
Expired items can be reclaimed by background maintenance, lookup, or allocation. The
server does not need to synchronously clear every expired byte at the TTL boundary.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Eviction" eyebrow="Live item removed" icon="warning" tone="rose" %}
When a class needs a chunk and cannot reclaim expired space or another page, it can
remove a non-expired item. An eviction is a capacity signal, not source-data loss.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

### Diagnose pressure by class, not only total RAM

- Track `evictions`, `reclaimed`, current items, bytes, and hit/miss counters over the
  same time window.
- Inspect slab-class statistics to find a pressured object-size band beside unused
  capacity in another class.
- Review key and value size distributions before changing the slab growth factor or
  maximum item size.
- Avoid storing very large payloads that increase serialization time, transfer cost,
  allocator waste, and blast radius per miss.
- Prefer compact, independently refreshable objects over one giant value when readers
  do not always need the whole payload.

The [official performance guide](https://docs.memcached.org/serverguide/performance/)
explains current slab allocation, reclaim, and LRU behavior in more detail.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="amber" %}
## Design keys, TTLs, and invalidation as one contract

A cache key is a compatibility boundary. It should identify the representation, owner,
and schema version without exposing secrets or becoming so long that key memory
dominates small values.

{% process-flow %}
{% process-step number="1" title="Namespace the owner" label="Collision control" %}
Begin with the service and entity, such as `catalog:product`, so unrelated teams cannot
accidentally reuse the same key space.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="2" title="Include representation version" label="Schema change" %}
Add a version such as `v3` when serialization or derived fields change. Old keys can
expire naturally instead of requiring a dangerous global flush.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="3" title="Choose freshness" label="TTL and invalidation" %}
Derive TTL from acceptable staleness and source recovery cost. Invalidate or move the
version after authoritative writes when waiting for TTL would violate the contract.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="4" title="Bound refill ownership" label="Concurrency" %}
Choose single-flight, a short lease, early recache, or stale serving so a popular key
does not multiply source work at expiration.
{% /process-step %}
{% /process-flow %}

{% code-block file="/api/content/technology/memcached/code/cache-aside-with-guard.py" language="python" title="Use a versioned key and single-flight refill" /%}

### Match invalidation to the data contract

- **TTL only:** suitable when bounded staleness is acceptable and writes do not require
  immediate visibility.
- **Delete after source commit:** simple for cache-aside, but a failed delete leaves the
  old value until TTL and a racing refill can repopulate stale data.
- **Versioned keys:** useful for schema releases and immutable source versions; old
  copies become unreachable and expire later.
- **CAS-guarded updates:** reject a write if another caller changed the cached item
  after it was read.
- **Event-driven invalidation:** reduce stale windows across writers, but make delivery,
  replay, deduplication, and ordering observable.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="rose" %}
## Make node loss and refill races visible

Memcached servers are independent. A lost node removes its resident values, and a pool
change can send keys to new nodes. The cache itself does not coordinate a database-safe
refill or reject a stale value that finishes last.

Use the lab to compare routing, per-key refill ownership, and guarded writes. Each
control protects a different boundary: consistent hashing limits key movement,
single-flight limits duplicate source work, and CAS or versioned keys prevent an old
refill from replacing a newer value.

{% interactive-block id="technology/memcached-failure-lab" dataFile="/api/content/technology/memcached/data/failure-scenarios.json" /%}

### Test the failure as a source-capacity event

- Remove one cache node and measure the share of keys that move under the real client
  continuum or proxy policy.
- Restart the entire pool and confirm source admission control holds while hit rate is
  near zero.
- Expire a hot key under peak concurrency and verify that only the intended refill
  owners reach the source.
- Delay an older source read until after a newer read completes; confirm the cache does
  not end with the older version.
- Fail cache connections slowly as well as immediately. A long cache timeout can make
  fallback slower than bypassing the cache.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="blue" %}
## Use commands and protocols deliberately

The basic text protocol exposes a small command set. New clients should also evaluate
the **meta text protocol**, which the current Memcached documentation recommends over
the deprecated binary protocol.

{% concept-grid columns="3" %}
{% concept-card title="Read and write values" eyebrow="get / set / delete" icon="database" tone="blue" %}
`get` returns one or more present values. `set` writes regardless of prior existence;
`add` writes only when absent. `delete` invalidates a key but cannot coordinate an
external database transaction.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Guard concurrent updates" eyebrow="gets / cas" icon="shield" tone="violet" %}
`gets` returns a CAS token. `cas` stores only if that token still matches, allowing a
client to detect an intervening cache update instead of blindly overwriting it.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Coordinate modern refill" eyebrow="Meta protocol" icon="bolt" tone="green" %}
Meta commands can expose TTL and item metadata, elect a recache winner, support early
recache, and intentionally serve stale data while revalidation runs.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

### Avoid protocol surprises

- Basic text keys are limited to 250 bytes and cannot contain spaces or newlines.
- A basic expiration value of `0` means no expiry, but the item can still be evicted or
  disappear with its node.
- `incr` and `decr` operate on an existing unsigned integer representation; they do not
  create a missing counter.
- `flush_all` invalidates items and is not an ordinary deployment or schema-migration
  tool.
- Avoid ASCII `noreply` for general use because a client cannot reliably align mutation
  errors with individual requests.

See the official [protocol overview](https://docs.memcached.org/protocols/),
[basic text reference](https://docs.memcached.org/protocols/basic/), and
[meta protocol guide](https://docs.memcached.org/protocols/meta/) for the exact wire
contract supported by the deployed version and client.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="green" %}
## Operate the cache as a dependency, not a black box

Hit rate alone can improve while the system becomes less safe. A smaller hot set may
raise hit rate while one node saturates, or a source slowdown may make the remaining
misses unaffordable.

{% metric-strip %}
{% metric label="Outcome" value="hits / misses" detail="Split by operation, tenant, key family, and node" tone="blue" /%}
{% metric label="Memory" value="bytes / evictions" detail="Inspect slab classes and object-size distributions" tone="violet" /%}
{% metric label="Latency" value="p50 / p95 / p99" detail="Separate cache, source fallback, and end-to-end time" tone="amber" /%}
{% metric label="Failure" value="timeouts / reconnects" detail="Track removals, remaps, cold starts, and source amplification" tone="rose" /%}
{% /metric-strip %}

### Build an operational review around consequences

- Alert on source load and user latency alongside hit rate; misses become dangerous
  only through their downstream cost.
- Track evictions and out-of-memory write failures by slab class and value-size family.
- Inventory client versions, hash-ring configuration, timeouts, retry behavior, and
  server lists across every application fleet.
- Roll node changes gradually and cap refill concurrency before expanding or shrinking
  a pool.
- Run cold-cache, one-node-loss, hot-key-expiry, slow-network, and stale-refill drills.
- Keep dashboards and logs free of full keys or values when they can reveal customer
  identifiers or sensitive payloads.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="violet" %}
## Secure the network and choose the right cache

Memcached should not be exposed to the public internet. Put it on a restricted network,
limit which workloads can connect, minimize sensitive values, and use encryption and
mutual authentication when the threat model requires them. Memcached supports stable
TLS in current releases when built and configured for it; verify client compatibility,
certificate validation, and performance with the
[official TLS guidance](https://docs.memcached.org/features/tls/).

{% concept-grid columns="3" %}
{% concept-card title="Choose Memcached" eyebrow="Disposable object cache" icon="check" tone="green" %}
Use it for simple, high-volume cache-aside reads where values are opaque, misses are
safe, and client-side placement plus application-owned coherence are acceptable.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Choose Redis or another data service" eyebrow="Richer server behavior" icon="layers" tone="amber" %}
Use a different system when the design depends on durable state, replication semantics,
server-side data structures, streams, transactions, or richer atomic workflows.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Choose a CDN or HTTP cache" eyebrow="Protocol-aware delivery" icon="network" tone="blue" %}
Use an HTTP-aware cache when validators, request headers, status codes, shared-cache
directives, geographic delivery, and purge behavior are the core contract.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

The final design question is not "How much RAM should Memcached have?" It is "Which
source work becomes optional on a hit, and can the application remain correct and
recoverable when every cached copy disappears?"
{% /section-card %}

